            <rel name="list" type="multinuc"/>
        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="40" relname="span">It is very important for a software program to be easy to use.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="1" relname="antithesis">If it is difficult to use or understand you will not have many users at all.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="44" relname="span">For example, Linux versus Microsoft.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="3" relname="elaboration">Microsoft caters to the users that really do not know too much about computers in the first place.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="45" relname="antithesis">Linux is a good system and may even be considered better by some,</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="45" relname="span">but all in all Microsoft is easier to use.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="46" relname="disjunction">It is easy to learn and has a Help tool where you can ask (type) specific questions</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="46" relname="disjunction">or you can refer to FAQs on their website.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="10" relname="means">Another thing is that once you learn how to use one Microsoft Operating System</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="42" relname="span">you will be able to figure out the others.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="12" relname="condition">For example if you have used Windows 98,</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="43" relname="span">you could figure out Windows XP.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="14" relname="condition">If a program is not efficient,</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="51" relname="span">then what is the purpose of using it?</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="51" relname="elaboration">The main reason for technology today is efficiency.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="52" relname="span">Memorability is important in that you would not have to go through a tutorial every time you use the system.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="16" relname="elaboration">It should be simple to use EVERY TIME.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="53" relname="span">Error recovery is important</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="20" relname="circumstance">because when learning, or even using, a system</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="55" relname="span">you can make mistakes.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="55" relname="elaboration">You have to be able to retrieve information that you accidentally delete, for example.</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="23" relname="condition">If a user is not satisfied with a program</segment>
        <segment id="23" parent="57" relname="span">he/she simply will just not use it.</segment>
        <segment id="24" parent="58" relname="span">They will just go find another program that will meet their needs.</segment>
        <segment id="25" parent="24" relname="means">This day in age with so many options that will not be hard to do.</segment>
        <segment id="26" parent="60" relname="span">With flexibility I believe a user should have options</segment>
        <segment id="27" parent="26" relname="elaboration">or they should be able to customize according to what they want and what will be easier for them to accomplish their tasks.</segment>
        <segment id="28" parent="63" relname="span">For instance, with Windows XP you can change the interface to the way that you want it.</segment>
        <segment id="29" parent="61" relname="span">You can have pictures of your family and/or friends as a wallpaper or a screensaver.</segment>
        <segment id="30" parent="31" relname="concession">This is not going to make using the software harder or easier,</segment>
        <segment id="31" parent="62" relname="span">but it is a little perk that some users must have.</segment>
        <segment id="32" parent="64" relname="span">Consistency is good with software that has a lot of versions.</segment>
        <segment id="33" parent="34" relname="condition">If you have a program and come out with another version,</segment>
        <segment id="34" parent="66" relname="span">it should have the same features as the first plus more.</segment>
        <segment id="35" parent="68" relname="span">To keep customers loyal you have to stay consistent.</segment>
        <segment id="36" parent="37" relname="condition">If they are used to getting certain features,</segment>
        <segment id="37" parent="67" relname="span">then the next version you take it out then you are likely to lose a customer.</segment>
